// lg-contracts — generated from src/lg-contracts-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Contracts Manager / Commercial Counsel. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-contracts"] = (QB["lg-contracts"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does a contracts manager do?", "**Why they ask:** the opening question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I manage the lifecycle of commercial contracts — drafting, reviewing, redlining, negotiating terms, and managing them through approval, execution and renewal. I balance protecting the company from risk with enabling the business to close deals efficiently. I work with sales, procurement, legal and the counterparty to land terms that are acceptable on risk and workable commercially. The goal is sound contracts that get signed without the deal stalling — risk management that enables business, not blocks it.\"\n\nTip: Manage contract lifecycle; protect the company while enabling deals.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the balance you manage?","Protecting the company from legal and commercial risk while not slowing down or killing good deals with over-caution."]}],
["Review", "How do you review and redline a contract?", "**Why they ask:** the core daily skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I read it against our standards and risk tolerance, focusing on the terms that matter most — scope, payment, liability, indemnity, termination, IP, confidentiality, warranties. I mark unacceptable terms, propose alternatives, and prioritize: I push hard on genuine risk issues and concede on minor points to keep the deal moving. I use a playbook of acceptable positions and fallbacks so I'm consistent and fast, and I flag anything unusual to legal.\"\n\nTip: Focus on high-risk terms; use a playbook; pick battles.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["What terms carry the most risk?","Liability and indemnity (who pays if things go wrong), limitation of liability, IP ownership, and termination rights."]}],
["Liability", "Explain limitation of liability and indemnification.", "**Why they ask:** the highest-stakes terms.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Limitation of liability caps how much a party can be liable for and often excludes certain damages (like consequential/indirect) — it protects you from catastrophic exposure, so I want a reasonable cap, ideally tied to contract value, and the right exclusions. Indemnification is one party agreeing to cover certain losses or claims the other suffers — I scrutinize what we're agreeing to indemnify and push to make it mutual and limited. These two clauses determine who bears the financial risk when something goes wrong, so they get the most attention.\"\n\nTip: LoL caps exposure; indemnity shifts risk — both are top priority.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why cap liability at contract value?","To avoid exposure far larger than what you're earning from the deal — unlimited liability is a dealbreaker risk."]}],
["Negotiation", "How do you negotiate contract terms?", "**Why they ask:** core skill.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I understand what matters most to each side, know our must-haves versus nice-to-haves, and trade concessions on lower-priority points to win the ones that matter. I explain the business reason behind our positions rather than just saying no, stay professional and solution-oriented, and know my walk-away points. The goal is a deal both sides can live with — being reasonable and clear about genuine risk gets to signature faster than being rigid.\"\n\nTip: Know must-haves vs nice-to-haves; trade and explain the why.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle a counterparty's aggressive redlines?","Separate real risk issues from posturing, explain our reasoning, propose balanced alternatives, and hold firm on genuine dealbreakers."]}],
["Types", "What types of commercial contracts do you handle?", "**Why they ask:** breadth.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Commonly MSAs (master service agreements) and their SOWs, NDAs, vendor and procurement contracts, SaaS and licensing agreements, sales agreements, and partnership or reseller deals. Each has typical risk areas — SaaS cares about data, uptime SLAs and IP; procurement about delivery and liability. Knowing the common structures and where the real risks sit in each type lets me review efficiently and spot what matters.\"\n\nTip: MSAs/SOWs, NDAs, SaaS, procurement, sales — know each one's risk areas.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the difference between an MSA and an SOW?","The MSA sets the overarching legal terms; each SOW defines the specific scope, deliverables and price for a project under it."]}],
["Playbook", "What is a contract playbook and why use one?", "**Why they ask:** scaling contract work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A playbook documents our standard positions, acceptable fallback language, and approval thresholds for key terms — so reviewers are consistent, fast, and know when to escalate. It lets routine contracts move quickly without legal reviewing every word, while flagging the genuinely risky deviations. It scales the function and keeps risk positions consistent across the company, which is especially valuable as deal volume grows.\"\n\nTip: Standard positions and fallbacks for speed, consistency and escalation.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["When should a deviation escalate to legal?","When it exceeds the playbook's acceptable fallbacks on a material risk term — those need legal judgment."]}],
["Process", "How do you manage contract workflow and avoid bottlenecks?", "**Why they ask:** contracts can stall deals.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Clear intake, prioritization by deal urgency and value, a CLM (contract lifecycle management) system to track status and store contracts, templates and playbooks for speed, and proactive communication with stakeholders. Contracts are often the last step before revenue, so a slow process costs the business real money. I move fast on the routine, focus deep review where risk warrants, and keep deals from dying in legal review.\"\n\nTip: Triage by value/urgency; CLM, templates, fast turnaround.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What does a CLM system do?","Manages contracts through their lifecycle — intake, drafting, approval, signature, storage, and renewal tracking — with visibility."]}],
["Risk", "How do you decide which contract risks are acceptable?", "**Why they ask:** judgment is the job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I weigh the likelihood and severity of the risk against the deal's value and strategic importance, and against our risk tolerance. Not every unfavorable term is worth fighting — I accept low-probability or low-impact risks to close good deals and push hard on terms that could cause serious harm. I make the risk visible to the business owner when it's a judgment call, so it's a conscious decision, not a hidden one.\"\n\nTip: Weigh likelihood × severity vs deal value; surface judgment calls.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Who decides on a significant accepted risk?","The business owner, informed by me on the real risk — I advise and make it visible; I don't hide it or decide unilaterally."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a tough contract negotiation.", "**Why they ask:** real experience.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"A key customer demanded unlimited liability and broad indemnity — a serious risk. Rather than just refuse, I explained why it was a dealbreaker, proposed a reasonable liability cap with carve-outs for the things they truly cared about (like data breach), and made the indemnity mutual. We closed with balanced terms that protected us and addressed their real concern. Understanding what they actually needed unlocked it.\"\n\nTip: Show you solved a dealbreaker by addressing the real concern.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["What was the key to the compromise?","Finding what they genuinely needed (protection on data) and giving that specifically, instead of accepting unlimited risk everywhere."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you work with sales and the business?", "**Why they ask:** contracts serve the business.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"As a partner, not a blocker — I understand the deal's importance and timeline, respond fast, explain risks in business terms, and find ways to get to yes where I can. When sales sees legal/contracts as helping close deals rather than obstructing them, they involve us earlier, which leads to better outcomes. I protect the company while being genuinely enabling, and that reputation makes the whole process smoother.\"\n\nTip: Be an enabling partner; respond fast, explain in business terms.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why does being seen as enabling matter?","Sales involves you earlier and follows the process when they trust you'll help close deals, not just say no."]}],
["Closing", "Why contracts management?", "**Why they ask:** motivation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I enjoy the mix of legal analysis, negotiation and business impact — contracts are where risk and commercial reality meet, and getting them right protects the company while enabling it to grow. It's detail-oriented, strategic, and I like being the person who gets deals done soundly.\"\n\nTip: Legal rigor + negotiation + direct business impact.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Are you a lawyer?","Be honest — contracts managers may or may not be attorneys; commercial counsel are. State your background and training."]}]
]);

// Contracts Manager / Commercial Counsel — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-contracts"] = (QB["lg-contracts"] || []).concat([
["Clauses", "What are the key clauses you look at first in any commercial contract?", "**Why they ask:** they want to know you can triage a contract for the parts that carry the risk.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I go straight to the clauses that allocate money and risk: the scope and deliverables, price and payment terms, term and termination, limitation of liability, indemnification, warranties, IP ownership, and confidentiality and data protection. Those are where a deal goes wrong at scale. The business terms — what we're buying or selling and for how much — have to be right, but the risk-shifting clauses are where a problem turns into a large, unexpected liability. I read the whole thing, but I spend my attention proportional to the risk, not evenly across the boilerplate.\"\n\nTip: Triage to scope, payment, term/termination, liability cap, indemnity, warranties, IP, and confidentiality — the clauses that allocate money and risk.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why focus attention on the risk-allocation clauses?","That's where a dispute becomes a large, unexpected liability — the boilerplate rarely bankrupts you, an uncapped indemnity can."]}],
["Indemnity", "Explain indemnification, including defend and hold harmless.", "**Why they ask:** indemnity is one of the highest-stakes clauses in any contract.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An indemnity is a promise to cover the other party's losses from specified events — typically third-party claims. 'Defend' means actually funding and managing the legal defense of a claim, not just reimbursing afterward, which matters because defense costs pile up before any judgment. 'Hold harmless' means protecting them from liability for those losses. The negotiation is over scope: what triggers it, whether it's capped, and whether it's mutual. I pay close attention to what we're agreeing to indemnify — an indemnity for 'any claim arising from the agreement' is far broader and riskier than one limited to, say, our IP infringement or gross negligence.\"\n\nTip: Indemnity covers specified (usually third-party) losses; 'defend' funds the defense up front; scope and triggers are the whole negotiation.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is the duty to defend significant beyond indemnifying?","Defense costs accrue immediately and can exceed the eventual judgment — agreeing to defend means funding the fight, not just paying a final loss."]}],
["Liability", "How does a limitation of liability clause work, and what carve-outs matter?", "**Why they ask:** the LoL clause is often the single most negotiated term.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It caps a party's total liability — often at fees paid over some period — and usually waives indirect or consequential damages like lost profits. The cap is what protects you from a catastrophic award on a modest deal. The fight is over the carve-outs: things excluded from the cap, which commonly include indemnification obligations, breach of confidentiality, IP infringement, and gross negligence or willful misconduct. A cap with a broad indemnity carved out above it can leave you effectively uncapped, so I read the cap and the carve-outs together, not in isolation, and make sure the net exposure matches the deal's value.\"\n\nTip: The cap plus the consequential-damages waiver limit exposure; carve-outs (indemnity, confidentiality, IP, gross negligence) can reopen it — read them together.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why read the cap and carve-outs together?","A low cap means little if a broad indemnity sits carved out above it — the real exposure is the cap plus everything excluded from it."]}],
["Warranties", "What's the difference between representations and warranties, and how do you handle them?", "**Why they ask:** these shape what each party is promising and the remedies if it's false.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Loosely, a representation is a statement of fact made to induce the other party to enter the contract, and a warranty is a promise that a statement is and will remain true, with a contractual remedy if it's not. In practice they're often bundled as 'representations and warranties.' What I focus on is their substance and scope: what exactly is being promised — that the software performs to spec, that there's no IP infringement, that the party has authority to sign — and whether they're qualified by knowledge or materiality. I make sure we can actually stand behind what we're warranting and that what we receive from the other side covers our real concerns.\"\n\nTip: Focus on substance and scope — what's promised, and whether it's qualified by knowledge or materiality — and only warrant what you can stand behind.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why does a 'to our knowledge' qualifier matter?","It limits a warranty to what the party actually knows, so they're not liable for hidden facts they couldn't reasonably have known — a common, important hedge."]}],
["Termination", "What termination rights do you look for, and what's the difference between for-cause and for-convenience?", "**Why they ask:** exit rights determine how trapped you are in a bad deal.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Termination for cause lets a party exit on the other's material breach, usually after notice and a cure period. Termination for convenience lets a party exit without a reason on notice — valuable to the customer who wants flexibility, risky to the supplier who invested in the deal. I look at who has which rights, the notice and cure periods, and critically what happens on termination: payment for work done, return or deletion of data, transition assistance, and which obligations survive. An imbalanced termination clause — they can leave anytime but we're locked in — is exactly the kind of asymmetry I negotiate.\"\n\nTip: For-cause needs breach plus notice/cure; for-convenience needs none — check who holds each, the notice periods, and what happens on exit (payment, data, transition, survival).", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What post-termination terms matter most?","Payment for work done, return or deletion of data, transition assistance, and which obligations survive — the exit mechanics, not just the right to exit."]}],
["IP", "How do you handle intellectual property ownership and license grants in a contract?", "**Why they ask:** who owns and who can use the IP is central to tech and services deals.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I establish background IP — what each party brings — stays with its owner, then negotiate who owns what's created under the contract. In services or development deals this is pivotal: does the customer own the deliverables outright, or get a license while the provider keeps the underlying tools? For a license I scrutinize the scope — exclusive or not, perpetual or term, territory, sublicensing, and whether it survives termination — because a license that's too narrow is useless and one that's too broad gives away value. The principle is that IP rights match what each side actually needs and paid for, with no accidental give-aways.\"\n\nTip: Background IP stays with its owner; negotiate ownership vs license for new IP, and scrutinize license scope (exclusivity, term, territory, sublicense, survival).", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why might a provider grant a license rather than assign ownership?","So it keeps reusable tools and methods for other clients while the customer gets the rights it needs — assigning everything would give away the provider's core assets."]}],
["Confidentiality", "What do you watch for in confidentiality and NDA terms?", "**Why they ask:** confidentiality obligations are ubiquitous and easy to get wrong.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check the definition of confidential information isn't so broad it's unworkable or so narrow it misses what matters, and that the standard exclusions are there — information that's public, already known, or independently developed. I look at the permitted uses, who it can be shared with, the obligations on return or destruction, and the duration, since some information warrants perpetual protection (like trade secrets) while most has a defined term. Whether it's mutual or one-way should match the actual flow of information. The goal is real protection for genuinely sensitive information without agreeing to obligations we can't practically honor.\"\n\nTip: Check the definition, standard exclusions, permitted use/recipients, return/destruction, duration, and mutuality — real protection you can actually honor.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why do standard exclusions matter in an NDA?","Without them you could be liable for 'disclosing' information that was already public or that you independently developed — the exclusions keep the obligation fair and workable."]}],
["Privacy", "How do data protection and privacy terms affect your contract review?", "**Why they ask:** data processing terms carry major regulatory and liability risk.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"When a contract involves personal data, I make sure the data-protection terms — often a data processing addendum — are present and correct: what data is processed, for what purpose, the roles of each party, security obligations, breach notification timelines, sub-processor controls, and data-return or deletion on termination. These tie to privacy laws, and getting them wrong creates both regulatory exposure and liability if there's a breach. I coordinate with privacy or security specialists on the technical requirements rather than pretend to expertise I don't have, and I make sure the contract's liability and indemnity treatment of a data breach is deliberate, since it's often carved out of the liability cap.\"\n\nTip: Ensure a correct DPA (scope, roles, security, breach notice, sub-processors, deletion) and check how a data breach is treated in liability and indemnity.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why coordinate with privacy or security specialists?","The technical and regulatory requirements are specialized; the contracts role is ensuring they're properly reflected in the terms, not improvising security expertise."]}],
["Payment", "What do you focus on in payment terms and pricing provisions?", "**Why they ask:** payment terms drive cash flow and dispute risk.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check the amounts, the schedule and triggers for payment, net terms, and what happens on late payment — interest, suspension rights, or dispute procedures. I look for how price changes are handled over a multi-year term — fixed, indexed, or capped increases — and whether there are hidden escalators or auto-uplifts. For the buyer I want clear acceptance criteria tied to payment so we pay for what we actually received; for the seller I want prompt, secure payment and remedies for non-payment. Ambiguity here is a frequent source of disputes, so I make the who-pays-what-when unambiguous.\"\n\nTip: Pin down amounts, triggers, net terms, late-payment remedies, and how price changes over the term — ambiguity here breeds disputes.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why tie payment to acceptance criteria for a buyer?","So the buyer pays for deliverables that actually meet spec, rather than paying on a date regardless of whether the work was acceptable."]}],
["SLA", "How do you evaluate service levels and service credits in a services or SaaS contract?", "**Why they ask:** SLAs are where service quality becomes contractually enforceable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check that the SLA measures what actually matters to the business — uptime, response and resolution times — with clear definitions, measurement methods, and exclusions, because a vague SLA is unenforceable. Service credits are the usual remedy for misses, but I'm realistic that credits are often small relative to the business impact of an outage, so for critical services I look at whether chronic failure gives a termination right, not just a credit. I make sure the SLA's exclusions (scheduled maintenance, customer-caused issues) are reasonable and that the reporting lets us actually verify performance rather than taking the provider's word.\"\n\nTip: Ensure SLAs measure what matters with clear definitions and verifiable reporting; credits are a weak remedy, so secure termination rights for chronic failure.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why aren't service credits enough protection on their own?","Credits are usually a small fraction of the business impact of an outage — for critical services you also want a termination right for chronic or severe failures."]}],
["Force majeure", "What is a force majeure clause and what do you check in it?", "**Why they ask:** force majeure drew intense scrutiny after recent global disruptions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Force majeure excuses or suspends a party's performance when a defined extraordinary event beyond its control prevents it — things like natural disasters, war, or government action. I check the list of triggering events and whether it's exhaustive or illustrative, what the affected party must do (notice, mitigation efforts), whether payment obligations are still excused, and when the other party can terminate if the disruption drags on. Recent disruptions taught everyone to read it carefully — whether events like pandemics or supply shocks are actually covered depends on the specific wording, so I make sure it reflects the risks this deal genuinely faces rather than leaving it as unread boilerplate.\"\n\nTip: Check the triggering events, notice and mitigation duties, whether payment is excused, and the termination right for prolonged events — tailor it, don't leave it as boilerplate.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why did force majeure get more scrutiny recently?","Major disruptions tested whether events like pandemics or supply shocks were actually covered — and the answer depended entirely on the specific wording, exposing lazy boilerplate."]}],
["Disputes", "How do you approach governing law, jurisdiction, and dispute resolution clauses?", "**Why they ask:** where and how disputes get resolved can decide their outcome.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Governing law sets which jurisdiction's law interprets the contract; the forum or jurisdiction clause sets where disputes are heard; and the dispute-resolution clause chooses the mechanism — courts, arbitration, or a tiered process starting with negotiation or mediation. These seem like boilerplate but matter a lot: litigating far away under unfavorable law is expensive and disadvantageous, and arbitration trades court rights for speed and privacy, which cuts both ways. I push for a neutral or favorable governing law and forum, consider whether arbitration suits the relationship, and like a tiered escalation so minor disputes get resolved before they become litigation.\"\n\nTip: Governing law, forum, and mechanism (court vs arbitration, with tiered escalation) shape cost and outcome — don't treat them as throwaway boilerplate.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a trade-off of arbitration versus court?","Arbitration is often faster and private with limited appeal, but it forgoes some procedural rights and can be costly — whether it helps depends on the relationship and dispute types."]}],
["Clauses", "How do you handle assignment and change-of-control provisions?", "**Why they ask:** these control what happens if a counterparty is acquired or transfers the deal.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Assignment clauses govern whether a party can transfer its rights and obligations to someone else, usually requiring the other party's consent. Change-of-control provisions address what happens if a party is acquired — sometimes triggering a consent right or a termination right. I care about these because I don't want to end up locked into a contract with a competitor or an unknown party after the counterparty sells, and conversely I want flexibility for our own corporate changes. Typically I'll allow assignment to affiliates or in a genuine merger but require consent otherwise, balancing our flexibility against control over who we're actually in business with.\"\n\nTip: Control who the deal can transfer to — allow affiliate/merger assignment but require consent otherwise, so you don't end up contracted to a competitor or unknown.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why include a change-of-control trigger?","So being acquired by, say, a competitor can require consent or allow termination — you keep a say over who you're ultimately doing business with."]}],
["Insurance", "Why do contracts require insurance, and what do you verify?", "**Why they ask:** insurance requirements back up indemnities with real dollars.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Insurance requirements make sure the other party can actually pay if their indemnity or liability is triggered — an indemnity from a party with no assets or coverage is worthless. So I check the required types and limits match the risk — general liability, professional liability or E&O for services, cyber for data, workers' comp — and that they're reasonable for the deal size. I look for additional-insured status where appropriate, a requirement to provide certificates, and notice of cancellation. The insurance clause and the liability and indemnity clauses work together: the insurance is what turns a promise to pay into an ability to pay.\"\n\nTip: Insurance makes indemnities collectible — verify the types and limits fit the risk, additional-insured status, certificates, and cancellation notice.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How does the insurance clause relate to indemnification?","Indemnity is the promise to pay; insurance is the means to pay it — adequate coverage is what makes an indemnity from a smaller counterparty actually worth something."]}],
["Restrictive", "How do you handle non-compete and non-solicitation clauses in commercial contracts?", "**Why they ask:** restrictive covenants carry enforceability and fairness issues.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"In a commercial context these might restrict a party from competing or from soliciting the other's customers or employees for a period. I focus on scope — duration, geography, and the activities restricted — because overly broad restraints can be unenforceable and are certainly bad faith, and on whether the restriction is genuinely necessary to protect a legitimate interest. Employee non-solicit between business partners is common and reasonable; a sweeping non-compete is harder to justify and enforce. Where enforceability law is involved I flag it for legal review rather than assume, since it varies significantly by jurisdiction and is an area under active change.\"\n\nTip: Scrutinize scope (duration, geography, activities) and legitimate interest; narrow non-solicits are reasonable, broad non-competes often aren't — and enforceability varies by jurisdiction.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why can an overly broad restrictive covenant backfire?","If it's unreasonable in scope it may be unenforceable, leaving you with no protection at all — a narrower, defensible restriction is often worth more than an aggressive one."]}],
["Services", "What do you look for in a statement of work and acceptance terms?", "**Why they ask:** vague scope and acceptance drive the most services disputes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The statement of work is where disputes are born or avoided, so I push for specific, measurable deliverables, timelines, responsibilities of each party, assumptions, and what's explicitly out of scope. Acceptance terms matter just as much: clear criteria, a defined review period, and what happens if a deliverable is rejected — rework, re-testing, and the effect on payment and schedule. Deemed-acceptance clauses, where silence means acceptance after a period, need a reasonable window. The principle is that both sides should be able to point to the SOW and agree on whether the work was delivered — ambiguity there is what ends up in a dispute.\"\n\nTip: Demand specific deliverables, responsibilities, and out-of-scope items, plus clear acceptance criteria, review period, and rejection remedies tied to payment.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why are acceptance criteria so important?","They define objectively whether the work was delivered, so payment and sign-off don't become a subjective argument — the number-one source of services disputes."]}],
["Boilerplate", "Why do order-of-precedence and entire-agreement clauses matter?", "**Why they ask:** so-called boilerplate can decide a dispute.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"With layered documents — a master agreement, order forms, SOWs, and exhibits — an order-of-precedence clause says which controls when they conflict, so you're not arguing later about which term wins. An entire-agreement or integration clause says the written contract is the complete deal, so prior emails, proposals, and verbal promises don't count unless they're in it. Both prevent surprises: I make sure precedence reflects what the parties actually intend to govern, and that anything important said during negotiations actually made it into the final document, because the integration clause will exclude it if it didn't.\"\n\nTip: Precedence resolves conflicts between layered documents; integration makes the written contract the whole deal — so get every important promise into the document.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of relying on a promise made in negotiation emails?","An entire-agreement clause means only what's in the signed contract counts — a promise left out of the document is generally unenforceable."]}],
["Renewal", "How do you handle auto-renewal or evergreen provisions?", "**Why they ask:** auto-renewals quietly lock companies into unwanted commitments.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Auto-renewal clauses renew the term automatically unless a party gives notice by a deadline, and evergreen terms continue indefinitely. The trap is missing the notice window and being locked in for another full term, often with a price increase. I flag these clearly, make sure the renewal terms and any price escalation are acceptable, and critically ensure we have a process to track the non-renewal notice deadlines — a calendar and owner — so a renewal is a conscious choice, not an accident. Where I can, I prefer shorter auto-renewal terms and reasonable notice windows so we're never trapped by a missed date.\"\n\nTip: Auto-renewals trap you if you miss the notice window — track non-renewal deadlines, vet renewal pricing, and prefer short terms with reasonable notice.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What operational control prevents an unwanted auto-renewal?","Tracking every non-renewal notice deadline with an owner and calendar reminder, so renewal is a deliberate decision rather than an accident of a missed date."]}],
["Compliance", "How do you address audit rights and compliance representations in contracts?", "**Why they ask:** audit and compliance terms manage ongoing risk after signing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Audit rights let one party verify the other's compliance — common in licensing (verifying usage) and outsourcing (verifying security or financial accuracy). I negotiate reasonable scope, notice, frequency, and who bears the cost, so an audit right isn't used as harassment but still provides real verification. On compliance reps, I make sure the contract includes the warranties and obligations the deal needs — things like compliance with applicable laws, and where relevant anti-bribery and export-control commitments — because these protect against the counterparty's misconduct creating liability for us. I align with specialists on regulated areas rather than drafting them from scratch.\"\n\nTip: Negotiate reasonable audit scope/notice/cost, and include the compliance warranties the deal needs (laws, anti-bribery, export) to guard against counterparty misconduct.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why include anti-bribery or export-control reps?","A counterparty's violation can create legal and reputational liability for you — contractual commitments and the right to terminate for breach are a key protection."]}]
]);

// Contracts Manager / Commercial Counsel — part 3. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["lg-contracts"] = (QB["lg-contracts"] || []).concat([
["NDA", "When do you use a mutual versus a one-way NDA?", "**Why they ask:** a basic but frequent judgment call.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"It should match the actual flow of information. A one-way NDA protects disclosures from one party only — appropriate when, say, we're sharing sensitive information with a vendor who isn't sharing anything comparable back. A mutual NDA protects both, which is right for an exploratory discussion, a potential partnership, or due diligence where both sides exchange confidential information. I default to matching reality rather than reflexively signing whichever the other side sent; a counterparty handing me a one-way NDA that only protects them, when we'll be sharing too, is a quick thing to flip to mutual.\"\n\nTip: Match the NDA to the real information flow — one-way for one-sided disclosure, mutual when both sides share.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why not just sign the NDA the other side sends?","It may only protect them while you're also disclosing — check whether the direction of protection matches reality and flip it to mutual if needed."]}],
["Structure", "Why structure a relationship as a master agreement with separate SOWs or order forms?", "**Why they ask:** contract architecture affects speed and risk over a long relationship.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A master services agreement holds the legal terms that rarely change — liability, indemnity, IP, confidentiality — negotiated once, while individual statements of work or order forms handle the specific scope, pricing, and timeline of each engagement. The payoff is speed and consistency: after the MSA is signed, each new project only needs a short SOW rather than renegotiating the whole legal framework every time. It also keeps risk terms consistent across all the work. I make sure the SOWs properly incorporate the MSA and that the order of precedence is clear, so a business term in an SOW doesn't accidentally override a key legal protection in the master.\"\n\nTip: The MSA negotiates legal terms once; SOWs/order forms add scope and price fast — set precedence so an SOW can't override key MSA protections.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's a risk of the MSA/SOW structure?","An SOW can inadvertently contradict or override the master's protections — a clear order-of-precedence clause prevents the specific document from quietly undoing key legal terms."]}],
["SaaS", "What's specific about reviewing a SaaS or subscription agreement?", "**Why they ask:** software subscriptions have distinctive risk points.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Beyond the usual terms, I focus on the things unique to subscription software: uptime SLAs and credits, data ownership and the right to get your data back on exit, security and privacy commitments, the subscription term and auto-renewal, and how fees change at renewal. I check usage limits and overage pricing, what happens to data on termination, and whether the provider can unilaterally change terms or features. The liability cap on a SaaS deal is often tied to fees, which can be small relative to the damage of an outage or breach, so I look hard at the cap, the data-breach treatment, and the business-continuity and exit provisions.\"\n\nTip: Focus on uptime SLAs, data ownership and portability, security/privacy, auto-renewal and fee changes, usage/overage, and the liability cap versus breach/outage impact.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why scrutinize data return and deletion on a SaaS exit?","Your business data lives in the provider's system — without clear rights to retrieve it and require deletion, you can be stranded or exposed when the subscription ends."]}],
["Negotiation", "A vendor insists you use their paper and won't accept redlines. How do you respond?", "**Why they ask:** leverage and creativity in constrained negotiations.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I don't accept 'take it or leave it' at face value, but I pick my battles. I'll identify the few truly unacceptable terms — an uncapped liability, a one-sided indemnity, giving away our IP — and press hard on those with a clear business rationale, while conceding the minor points to show good faith. If they genuinely won't redline, I can sometimes get protection through a short addendum or side letter rather than editing their template. And I'm honest with my business stakeholders about the residual risk if we proceed on their paper, so it's a conscious decision. Leverage matters too: a vendor we're a large customer to will usually find flexibility they claimed not to have.\"\n\nTip: Concede minor points, press only the deal-breakers with a business rationale, use an addendum if they won't redline, and surface residual risk for a conscious decision.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you get protection if they truly won't edit their template?","A short addendum or side letter that overrides specific terms can achieve the key protections without redlining their base paper, if an order-of-precedence is set."]}],
["Authority", "What is delegation of authority, and why does it matter for contract signing?", "**Why they ask:** signing the wrong contract at the wrong level creates real exposure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Delegation of authority defines who in the organization can approve and sign contracts, usually by type, value, and risk — a routine low-value order might be signed by a manager, while a high-value or high-risk deal requires senior or executive sign-off. It matters because a contract signed by someone without authority creates confusion about enforceability and, more practically, means risk wasn't reviewed at the right level. I make sure contracts route through the correct approvals before signature, that signatories have authority, and that the approval thresholds actually match risk, not just dollar value — a small-dollar deal with an uncapped liability still deserves senior eyes.\"\n\nTip: DOA sets who can sign by value, type, and risk — route approvals correctly, and gate on risk not just dollar amount.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why gate approvals on risk, not just contract value?","A low-dollar deal can carry high risk — an uncapped indemnity or data exposure — so approval thresholds should catch dangerous terms regardless of the price tag."]}],
["Process", "How does contract lifecycle management software and a central repository improve the function?", "**Why they ask:** mature contract operations run on systems, not inboxes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A CLM system and searchable repository turn contracts from scattered files into a managed asset. It centralizes executed contracts so I can actually find terms, track key dates like renewals and expirations, enforce approval workflows and templates, and report on cycle times and obligations. The biggest practical wins are never missing a renewal or notice deadline again, and being able to answer 'which of our contracts have this clause' when a new risk emerges — something impossible with contracts buried in email. I'd implement it with clean metadata and discipline, because a repository is only as useful as the data and process behind it.\"\n\nTip: CLM centralizes contracts for findability, deadline tracking, workflow, and reporting — its value depends on clean metadata and consistent process.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the biggest operational risk a repository eliminates?","Missed renewal and notice deadlines, and the inability to find which contracts contain a given clause when a new risk or question arises."]}],
["Metrics", "What metrics do you track to run the contracts function well?", "**Why they ask:** contracts is increasingly managed as an operation with KPIs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Cycle time — how long from request to signature — because the business feels contract delays as lost deals or stalled projects, and it's where I can add visible value by speeding things up without cutting corners. I also track volume and backlog, the mix of standard versus heavily negotiated contracts, where deals get stuck in the approval chain, and renewal and obligation tracking. The goal of the metrics is to find bottlenecks and improve them — if most delay is in one approval step, I fix that step. I'm careful that speed metrics never become an excuse to skip real risk review, so I pair them with quality checks.\"\n\nTip: Track cycle time, backlog, standard-vs-negotiated mix, and approval bottlenecks — to speed the process without sacrificing risk review.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you keep a speed metric from undermining quality?","Pair cycle-time targets with quality and risk checks, and attack bottlenecks (slow approval steps) rather than pressuring reviewers to skim high-risk terms."]}],
["Prioritization", "You have a backlog of contracts to review. How do you triage?", "**Why they ask:** contracts managers are perpetually over capacity.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I triage by risk and business urgency, not first-in-first-out. A high-value or high-risk deal, or one blocking revenue or a deadline, jumps the queue; a routine, low-risk standard-template contract can move fast or be self-served by the business with a playbook. I apply effort proportional to risk — a standard NDA gets a quick pass, a complex MSA with an offshore counterparty gets deep attention. I also communicate turnaround expectations so the business can plan, and I use templates and a self-service playbook to keep low-risk volume off my desk entirely, reserving my time for the deals where judgment actually matters.\"\n\nTip: Triage by risk and business urgency, apply effort proportional to risk, and push low-risk volume to templates and self-service.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you keep low-risk contracts off your desk?","Standard templates and a playbook let the business self-serve routine deals within set guardrails, reserving your review for the genuinely risky or high-value ones."]}],
["Resourcing", "How do you decide when to use outside counsel versus handle something in-house?", "**Why they ask:** cost and expertise trade-offs in legal work.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I keep in-house the high-volume, business-as-usual commercial work where I know our positions and risk tolerance better than anyone — that's the core value of an in-house contracts function. I bring in outside counsel for specialized expertise we don't have, like a novel regulatory question, litigation, a major M&A deal, or a jurisdiction's law I'm not qualified on. The decision balances cost, the stakes, and whether it's truly specialized or just unfamiliar. When I do engage outside counsel, I scope it tightly and manage it actively so the spend maps to value, rather than handing over a problem and a blank check.\"\n\nTip: Keep routine commercial work in-house; use outside counsel for specialized expertise, litigation, or major deals — scoped tightly and actively managed.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you control outside counsel cost?","Scope the engagement tightly, set budgets and expectations up front, and manage it actively — don't hand over an open-ended problem with a blank check."]}],
["Risk", "How do you decide when to escalate a contract risk rather than handle it yourself?", "**Why they ask:** knowing your limits and the escalation path is essential.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I escalate when a term exceeds my authority or the organization's risk appetite, when it involves a judgment that's genuinely a business or legal decision above my role, or when I'm outside my competence — a specialized regulatory or litigation question. The point of escalation isn't to offload responsibility; it's to get the right decision-maker involved when the stakes or the uncertainty warrant it. I escalate with a clear recommendation and the trade-offs, not just a problem, so the decision-maker can act quickly. Under-escalating a real risk to avoid looking unsure is far more dangerous than asking.\"\n\nTip: Escalate when a term exceeds your authority, risk appetite, or competence — with a clear recommendation and trade-offs, not just a problem.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How should you escalate a risk effectively?","Present the issue, the options, the trade-offs, and your recommendation — so the decision-maker can decide fast rather than having to re-investigate from scratch."]}],
["Breach", "How do you handle a situation where the other party breaches the contract?", "**Why they ask:** contracts managers advise the business through disputes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I establish the facts and what the contract actually says — is it truly a material breach, and what does the agreement provide: notice, a cure period, remedies, termination rights, limitation of liability. Then I think about the business goal, which is usually to get performance back on track and preserve the relationship if it's worth keeping, not necessarily to litigate. So I'd typically start with notice and a chance to cure, document everything carefully in case it escalates, and reserve rights so we don't accidentally waive them. Litigation or termination is a last resort weighed against cost, the relationship, and the strength of our position. The contract is the roadmap, but the decision is a business one.\"\n\nTip: Establish the facts and what the contract provides, usually give notice and cure, document and reserve rights — litigation is a last resort weighed against cost and relationship.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why 'reserve rights' when responding to a breach?","So accepting partial performance or continuing to work doesn't inadvertently waive your right to enforce the breach or claim remedies later."]}],
["Changes", "When do you amend an existing contract versus write a new one?", "**Why they ask:** managing changes cleanly avoids confusion and gaps.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For a discrete change to an existing, otherwise-working agreement — a price adjustment, extended term, added service — an amendment is cleaner: it preserves the negotiated terms and history and just changes the specific points, clearly referencing what it supersedes. I write a new contract when the deal is fundamentally different, when there have been so many amendments the document is a confusing patchwork, or when the old terms no longer fit. The thing I avoid is informal change — a side email that modifies the deal without a proper amendment — because it creates ambiguity about what actually governs. Changes get documented formally, through the amendment or change mechanism the contract specifies.\"\n\nTip: Amend for discrete changes to a working contract; rewrite when the deal is fundamentally different or buried in amendments — and never modify informally.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why avoid modifying a contract by email or verbally?","It creates ambiguity about what actually governs and may not be enforceable; formal amendments keep a clear, enforceable record of the current deal."]}],
["Review", "How does reviewing a contract you didn't draft differ from drafting your own?", "**Why they ask:** most review work is on the other side's paper.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"On someone else's paper I'm reading defensively, because it's drafted to favor them, so I look for what's missing as much as what's there — a liability cap that only protects them, an indemnity running one way, no termination right for us, a broad license grant. I read every definition, since the risk often hides in how a term is defined and then used elsewhere. Drafting my own, I control the structure and can build in our protections from the start. Reviewing their draft is about efficiently finding and fixing the asymmetries and gaps, prioritizing the few terms that carry real risk rather than rewriting their document into mine.\"\n\nTip: On the other side's paper, read defensively for one-sided terms and missing protections, check the definitions, and fix the few high-risk asymmetries.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why read the definitions so carefully in another party's draft?","Risk often hides in a defined term used throughout — a broadly defined 'confidential information' or 'services' can quietly expand obligations far beyond what the clause seems to say."]}],
["Judgment", "How do you balance legal risk against the business's need for speed and revenue?", "**Why they ask:** the core tension of a commercial contracts role.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I see my job as enabling the business to do deals safely, not blocking them, so I lead with 'how do we get to yes' rather than 'here's why we can't.' I distinguish the risks that genuinely matter — the ones that could cause real, material harm — from the theoretical or low-probability ones that aren't worth killing a deal over. For the real ones I'm firm and explain the stakes in business terms; for the minor ones I'm pragmatic. And where there's residual risk the business wants to accept for a good commercial reason, I make it an informed, documented decision by the right person rather than a unilateral no. Speed and safety aren't opposites if you're proportionate.\"\n\nTip: Enable deals safely — distinguish material from theoretical risk, be firm on the former and pragmatic on the latter, and let the business accept residual risk knowingly.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you avoid being seen as the department of 'no'?","Lead with how to get to yes, reserve firmness for genuinely material risks, and present accepted residual risk as an informed business decision rather than a blanket refusal."]}],
["Ethics", "As a non-lawyer contracts manager, how do you stay within the limits of your role?", "**Why they ask:** the line between contract management and practicing law matters.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'm clear about what I do and don't do. I manage contracts, apply the company's approved positions and playbook, negotiate commercial terms, and flag issues — but I don't give legal opinions I'm not qualified to give or make calls that require legal judgment I'm not licensed for. When something crosses into genuine legal analysis — enforceability, regulatory interpretation, litigation strategy — I route it to counsel rather than improvise. This protects the company and me: overstepping into legal advice I'm not qualified to give creates risk, and the right move is knowing the boundary and working closely with legal within it. Many contracts managers are highly skilled precisely because they know where that line is.\"\n\nTip: Manage contracts and apply approved positions, but route genuine legal judgment (enforceability, regulatory interpretation) to counsel — know the boundary.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What kinds of questions should go to a qualified lawyer?","Enforceability opinions, regulatory interpretation, litigation strategy, and jurisdiction-specific legal judgment — matters requiring a licensed lawyer's analysis rather than applying approved positions."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you caught a risky term that others missed.", "**Why they ask:** they want evidence of the vigilance the role requires.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"On a vendor agreement the business was eager to sign, I noticed the liability cap had an exception that effectively made our indemnification obligation unlimited, buried in a cross-reference. The business had read the headline cap and felt protected. I quantified the real exposure — potentially many times the contract value — explained it in plain business terms, and negotiated the carve-out down to a reasonable sub-cap. The deal still closed, just safely. The lesson I reinforce is to always read the cap and its exceptions together, because the dangerous risk is usually in the interaction between clauses, not any single one read alone.\"\n\nTip: Show you found risk hidden in a cross-reference or interaction, quantified it in business terms, and fixed it without killing the deal.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Where does serious contract risk most often hide?","In the interaction between clauses — a cap undercut by a carve-out, an indemnity widened by a broad definition — not in any single clause read in isolation."]}],
["Negotiation", "How do you think about leverage and your best alternative in a contract negotiation?", "**Why they ask:** understanding leverage drives realistic negotiation.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I go in knowing our leverage honestly — are we the big customer they need, or the small account they can walk from, and what's our realistic alternative if this deal doesn't happen. That best alternative anchors how hard I can push: strong alternatives let me hold firm on key terms, while a weak position means I prioritize ruthlessly and win the few things that matter most. I also try to understand the other side's pressures and alternatives, because leverage is relative. And I separate the terms we must have from the ones we'd like, so I spend my leverage on what protects us from real harm rather than scattering it across every clause.\"\n\nTip: Know your realistic alternative and relative leverage honestly, then spend it on the must-have terms that prevent real harm, not every clause.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What do you do when your leverage is weak?","Prioritize ruthlessly — concede the minor points and spend your limited leverage winning the few terms that protect against genuine material harm."]}],
["Renewal", "How do you approach renegotiating an existing contract at renewal?", "**Why they ask:** renewals are a key moment to improve terms and pricing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A renewal is leverage I shouldn't waste. Ahead of it I review how the relationship actually went — did the vendor meet SLAs, were there disputes, did our needs change — and identify terms worth improving: pricing, a liability cap that was too low, a one-sided clause we accepted under time pressure originally. I benchmark the pricing against the market so I'm negotiating from facts, and I use our continued business as the lever to fix the terms and price. I also watch the non-renewal notice deadline so I'm negotiating by choice, not scrambling because an auto-renewal is about to trigger. Renewal is the cheapest time to fix a deal.\"\n\nTip: Use the renewal as leverage — review performance, benchmark pricing, fix terms accepted under earlier pressure, and act before the auto-renewal deadline.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["Why is renewal often the best time to improve terms?","The vendor wants to keep the business, so continued spend is real leverage to fix pricing and one-sided terms that were accepted under original time pressure."]}],
["Program", "Why standardize on templates and a contract playbook, and how do you build one?", "**Why they ask:** scaling the function requires standardization.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Templates and a playbook are how a contracts function scales without dropping quality. Standard templates with pre-approved terms mean most deals start from a safe, consistent position, and a playbook — our preferred position, acceptable fallbacks, and the walk-away point for each key clause — lets reviewers, and even the business on low-risk deals, negotiate confidently and consistently without escalating everything. I build one by capturing the positions we actually take and why, keeping it practical and current, and tiering it by risk so routine deals move fast. The payoff is faster cycle times, consistent risk posture, and senior time reserved for the genuinely novel.\"\n\nTip: Templates and a tiered playbook (preferred position, fallbacks, walk-away) scale quality and speed — reserve senior review for the novel.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What makes a playbook actually get used?","Keeping it practical, current, and tiered by risk — if it's clear and reflects real positions, reviewers and the business follow it instead of escalating every point."]}],
["Closing", "What makes a great contracts manager or commercial counsel?", "**Why they ask:** self-awareness about the role's blend of skills.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Sharp attention to detail to catch the risk hiding in a cross-reference, combined with business judgment to know which risks actually matter and which to let go. Strong negotiation and communication, because the job is as much about getting to a deal and explaining risk in plain terms as it is about the drafting. And a service mindset — seeing the function as enabling the business to move fast and safely, not as a gatekeeper. The best contracts professionals are trusted by the business because they say yes thoughtfully and no rarely, and when they do push back, everyone knows it's for a reason that matters.\"\n\nTip: Detail plus business judgment, negotiation and plain-language communication, and a service mindset — enabling the business, not gatekeeping it.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["How do you earn the business's trust as a contracts professional?","By enabling deals and saying yes thoughtfully, reserving pushback for risks that truly matter — so when you do object, people know it's for a real reason."]}]
]);
